#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# kronecker_delta

#% text
# `kronecker_delta(n1, n2, ...)` gives 1 when all the arguments are equal and 0 when two of them are different numbers.

#% text
# `kronecker_delta(n)` gives 1 for 0 and 0 for other numbers.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Numbers are compared by value, exact or machine: `kronecker_delta(1/2, 0.5)` is 1. Two machine numbers that differ in the last bit are different (`0.1 + 0.2` and `0.3`).

#% item
# With symbolic arguments that may differ the call stays; equal arguments give 1, and arguments that differ by a nonzero number (`a` and `a + 1`) give 0.

#% item
# `kronecker_delta` is not listable: a list argument stays. It is the discrete delta of an index; for a continuous variable use `dirac_delta`, and for the sequence `1, 0, 0, ...` of a transform `discrete_delta`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
kronecker_delta(2, 2)
#% output
#= 1

#% cell
kronecker_delta(1, 2)
#% output
#= 0

#% cell
kronecker_delta(a, a)
#% output
#= 1

#% cell
kronecker_delta(i, j)
#% output
#= kronecker_delta(i, j)

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# The identity matrix as a table:

#% cell
table(kronecker_delta(i, j), i, range(1, 3), j, range(1, 3))
#% output
#= [[1, 0, 0], [0, 1, 0], [0, 0, 1]]

#% text
# Several arguments are all equal or not:

#% cell
[kronecker_delta(1, 1, 1), kronecker_delta(1, 1, 2), kronecker_delta(x, y, x)]
#% output
#= [1, 0, kronecker_delta(x, y, x)]

#% text
# One argument is compared with 0:

#% cell
[kronecker_delta(0), kronecker_delta(3)]
#% output
#= [1, 0]

#% text
# Exact, machine and complex numbers, and expressions that simplify to equal ones:

#% cell
[kronecker_delta(1/2, 0.5), kronecker_delta(im, im), kronecker_delta(2*a - a, a), kronecker_delta(a, a + 1)]
#% output
#= [1, 1, 1, 0]

#% text
# Large integers are compared exactly:

#% cell
kronecker_delta(10^100, 10^100)
#% output
#= 1

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The delta picks one term out of a sum:

#% cell
sum(kronecker_delta(k, 3)*k^2, [k, 1, 10])
#% output
#= 9

#% text
# A symbol that has a value is replaced before the comparison:

#% cell
kronecker_delta(i, j) /. [i -> 2, j -> 2]
#% output
#= 1

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The table of deltas is the identity matrix:

#% cell
identity_matrix(3) == table(kronecker_delta(i, j), i, range(1, 3), j, range(1, 3))
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# A list is not threaded; use `map`:

#% cell
kronecker_delta([1, 2, 3], 2)
#% output
#= kronecker_delta([1, 2, 3], 2)

#% cell
map(k => kronecker_delta(k, 2), [1, 2, 3])
#% output
#= [0, 1, 0]

#% text
# Machine numbers are compared as they are; rounding makes sums differ from their exact values:

#% cell
kronecker_delta(0.1 + 0.2, 0.3)
#% output
#= 0

#% text
# An exact and an arbitrary-precision number that cannot be compared stay a call:

#% cell
kronecker_delta(n(1/3, 30), 1/3)
#% output
#= kronecker_delta(0.3333333333333333333333333333333333, 1/3)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `discrete_delta`, `dirac_delta`, `identity_matrix`
